**New evidence**
- World Bank fiscal reform support
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Support Will Help Strengthen Indonesia’s Tax System, Improve Development Spending","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-24","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/24/new-support-will-help-strengthen-indonesia-tax-system-improve-development-spending","claim":"The World Bank approved a $750 million loan to help Indonesia increase tax revenue, improve tax system equity, and strengthen institutions for more efficient planning and spending; the package is framed as supporting tax policy and administration reforms.","locator":"Press release lead and paragraphs describing the two reform pillars","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release with specific financing and reform details; strong temporal fit to 2022 policy execution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and tied to Jokowi’s 2019-2024 administration.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level fiscal reform under Jokowi’s administration; not a personal act by Jokowi alone, but within executive policy control.","contrary_evidence":["World Bank notes Indonesia’s tax revenues were still low versus peers."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}
- World Bank human capital support
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Financing Will Support Indonesia to Improve and Protect Human Capital","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-07-05","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/07/05/new-financing-will-support-indonesia-to-improve-and-protect-human-capital","claim":"The World Bank approved a $350 million loan to support Indonesia’s human capital agenda by safeguarding income protection, increasing access to healthcare services, preventing unhealthy behavior, and improving the effectiveness of government spending; it also said Indonesia’s Human Capital Index improved from 0.50 to 0.54 between 2010 and 2020 but remained below peers.","locator":"Press release lead and human capital paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release with clear policy content, funding amount, and time fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 policy support and retrospective human-capital comparison relevant to the Jokowi period.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level reform agenda; attribution to Jokowi is indirect but real through executive control of social and fiscal policy.","contrary_evidence":["World Bank says education, social insurance, and stunting remained weak relative to peers."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}
- BLT distribution reached 95.9 percent
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Cash Direct Assistance Distribution for Fuel Oil Subsidy Reallocation Reaches up to 95.9 Percent","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-09-27","url":"https://setkab.go.id/en/cash-direct-assistance-distribution-for-fuel-oil-subsidy-reallocation-reaches-up-to-95-9-percent/","claim":"President Jokowi said BLT BBM distribution had reached 19.7 million beneficiaries, or 95.9 percent of the target, after the government reallocated fuel subsidies to maintain purchasing power.","locator":"Paragraph quoting the President after observing distribution in Baubau","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential statement with a specific implementation metric and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 implementation evidence within the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Jokowi’s announced policy shift and oversight of rollout, though execution was carried out by ministries and post offices.","contrary_evidence":["The measure is short-term relief; it does not prove long-run efficiency or equitable targeting by itself."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}
- APBN deficit below target
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Economic Minister: State Budget Deficit Smaller than Target","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-01-16","url":"https://setkab.go.id/en/economic-minister-state-budget-deficit-smaller-than-target/","claim":"The 2022 State Budget deficit was 2.38 percent of GDP, smaller than the target stated in law, and fiscal consolidation was achieved faster than planned.","locator":"Quoted statement by Coordinating Minister Airlangga Hartarto after the cabinet meeting led by President Jokowi","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government summary of a fiscal year outcome with a precise deficit figure and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on fiscal year 2022; directly relevant to Jokowi’s 2022 economic management.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Jokowi administration’s fiscal policy and cabinet coordination; not solely to Jokowi personally.","contrary_evidence":["Deficit performance partly benefited from stronger revenue conditions and cyclical recovery."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.7","8B.10"]}
- BPK audit of 2022 accounts
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"BPK Gives Unqualified Opinion on 2022 Financial Statements of Central Government","publisher":"BPK RI","publication_date":"2023-06-20","url":"https://www.bpk.go.id/news/bpk-gives-unqualified-opinion-on-2022-financial-statements-of-central-government","claim":"BPK gave an unqualified opinion on the central government’s 2022 financial statements; 81 of 82 line ministries and the state treasury statements also received unqualified opinions, and BPK said most fiscal-transparency criteria were met, while noting audit findings totaling Rp25.85 trillion.","locator":"Press release body and transparency paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary audit-board release on FY2022 accounts with concrete audit outcomes and transparency language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective FY2022 evidence; directly relevant to Jokowi’s administrative and fiscal management record.","ruler_attribution":"Government-wide financial management under Jokowi’s cabinet; the audit is not personal proof but is strong evidence of implementation quality.","contrary_evidence":["BPK also reported material findings and internal-control weaknesses, so the result is positive but not clean."],"lenses":["8B.5","8B.6","8B.10"]}
- UNICEF learning crisis
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"As children in Indonesia return to school, UNICEF calls for urgent action to address learning crisis","publisher":"UNICEF Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-07-13","url":"https://www.unicef.org/indonesia/press-releases/children-indonesia-return-school-unicef-calls-urgent-action-address-learning-crisis","claim":"UNICEF said extended school closures and disrupted reopenings affected around 60 million students in Indonesia and caused significant learning loss, with harms especially acute for vulnerable students.","locator":"First and second paragraphs of the press release","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF is a strong independent source on education disruptions, and the claim is specific and contemporaneous.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022, though the shock is partly inherited from the pandemic rather than uniquely Jokowi’s creation.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution to Jokowi is limited; this is mainly a constraint and implementation challenge for the administration’s recovery response rather than a ruler-caused collapse.","contrary_evidence":["The article primarily reflects pandemic inheritance; it does not isolate Jokowi’s personal policy failure."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}
- Poverty trajectory and residual insecurity
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Indonesia Poverty Assessment: Pathway Towards Economic Security","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-05-09","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/indonesia/publication/indonesia-poverty-assessment","claim":"The World Bank said Indonesia had made remarkable poverty-reduction progress and extreme poverty was basically eradicated by 2022, but more than one-third of Indonesians remained economically insecure and could be pushed into poverty by shocks; it urged broader opportunity creation, protection, and resilience investments.","locator":"Key messages section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent World Bank assessment with explicit 2022 retrospective and policy interpretation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 conditions and policy trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Broadly attributable to the Jokowi period, but the result reflects long-run reform, commodity conditions, and recovery as well as current administration choices.","contrary_evidence":["The assessment says economic insecurity remained high, so the progress is real but incomplete."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}
- IKN progress
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Presiden Jokowi Sampaikan Progres Pembangunan IKN","publisher":"Sekretariat Kabinet Republik Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-11-29","url":"https://setkab.go.id/presiden-jokowi-sampaikan-progres-pembangunan-ikn/","claim":"Jokowi said the government had started infrastructure work in the core Nusantara capital area, that the raw-water dam would be completed in January, and that ministry, presidential, and vice-presidential buildings would begin construction in December.","locator":"Lead paragraph and presidential quotes in the news item","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential update with direct statements on 2022 implementation milestones.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and tied to a flagship Jokowi program.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Jokowi as a personal flagship policy and executive priority, although much implementation was delegated to ministries and the IKN authority.","contrary_evidence":["This is initiation evidence, not completion; success depended on later delivery and financing."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.10"]}
- Immunization catch-up campaign
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Indonesia Targets Low Vaccination Areas to Tackle Decline in Childhood Immunization","publisher":"WHO Indonesia","publication_date":"2023-05-03","url":"https://www.who.int/indonesia/news-room/item/03-05-2023-indonesia-targets-low-vaccination-areas-to-tackle-decline-in-childhood-immunization","claim":"WHO said Indonesia achieved 94.6 percent full immunization coverage in 2022, exceeding the national target of 94.1 percent, and that the BIAN catch-up campaign reached 26.5 million children with measles-rubella vaccines, 1.3 million with polio vaccines, and 2 million with DTP-HB-Hib vaccines.","locator":"Opening paragraph and campaign summary","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO retrospective on 2022 national immunization performance with precise coverage and campaign figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 campaign and coverage; acceptable as target-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Jokowi’s administration through Ministry of Health coordination, though the campaign was operationally carried by health officials and local governments.","contrary_evidence":["WHO also noted outbreaks in low-vaccination areas, so the win was uneven and not fully durable."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across Setkab, Kemenkeu, BPS, Bank Indonesia, World Bank, IMF, WHO/UNICEF, BPK, Reuters/AP/CNA/Jakarta Post, and several duplicated translations of the same government releases. I kept only sources that added a materially distinct claim about Jokowi’s 2022 governing program, implementation, coordination, adaptation, or measurable outcomes.

Rejected or not separately accepted:
- Duplicate English/Indonesian versions of the same Setkab or WHO release when they added no new fact.
- Generic macro dashboards and country pages that repeated the same 2022 indicators without new ruler-specific linkage.
- Multiple stories on the June 2022 cabinet reshuffle that repeated the same facts; I kept one official explanation and one independent critical report as the best pair.
- Broad political-freedom or corruption materials that did not help 8B because they lacked program-to-implementation linkage.
- Post-2022 follow-ups that were only background unless they provided a clean retrospective on FY2022 outcomes.
- Source clusters around the same themes, such as subsidy reallocation, poverty, and inflation, when one authoritative official release plus one independent cross-check was enough.

**Remaining gaps**
- The strongest evidence is about program definition, budget/fiscal management, social assistance rollout, IKN initiation, and macro stabilization. Direct evidence on Jokowi personally supervising day-to-day implementation is thinner than evidence on the administration as a whole.
- Several favorable outcomes were aided by external tailwinds, especially commodity prices and post-pandemic reopening. That means outcome gains are not pure ruler skill signals.
- The record is weaker on whether underperforming implementers were systematically replaced across the whole program portfolio, beyond the June 2022 reshuffle and a few visible crisis responses.
- I did not find a clean, independent audit of Regsosek’s 2022 implementation quality by year-end; more search would help if you want deeper attribution on targeting and delivery.
- Education recovery remained incomplete, so additional source work on learning-loss remediation and subnational delivery would improve confidence in the human-capital part of the dossier.
